e52 R1 — the one rule this experiment recommends adopting.

Mention normalisation BEFORE the Elasticsearch query, in the single place that
builds the name query.  Nothing else in the pipeline changes: `res_formatter`
still measures edit distances against the ORIGINAL mention string (it is passed
`ex['search_name']`, not the query), so every candidate feature keeps meaning
what it meant at training time.  Cost is zero — the rule replaces one query
with another; it does not add one.

Guards live in `alias_targets` (lifted from experiments/e51_state_abbrev):
a bare two-letter code expands only when the whole mention is that code, in
caps, with no dots ("WA", "NC"), so "in LA" never becomes Louisiana; dotted AP
forms ("Ind.", "W.Va.", "N.M.") expand unconditionally.  Measured on all six
held-out sources: 65 firings in 8,977 mentions, 0 false expansions, 0 entities
made worse.

The `QUERY_OVERRIDE` line is load-bearing: `_clean_search_name` strips the
token "District", so expanding "D.C." to "District of Columbia" would send the
query "of Columbia".  The override sends "Washington, D.C." instead.

--- a/mordecai3/geonames.py
+++ b/mordecai3/geonames.py
@@
+from .place_aliases import alias_query
+
@@ class GeonamesService.build_name_search
         max_results = int(max_results)
         fuzzy = int(fuzzy)
+        # e52: a mention that is a US-state / Canadian-province abbreviation
+        # phrase-matches `alternativenames` and, under the alt_name_length
+        # sort, returns countries -- "WA" -> DR Congo, "Ky." -> the United
+        # Kingdom, "N.M." -> a Santa Fe hotel.  61% of all unretrievable golds
+        # in held-out TR/LGL/GWN are this one defect.  Expand before querying.
+        expanded = alias_query(search_name)
+        if expanded:
+            search_name = expanded
         search_name = _clean_search_name(search_name)

